          %h1#pure_sets Pure sets
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#motivation"} Motivation
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#formalisation_in_material_set_theory"} Formalisation in material set theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#formalization_in_structural_set_theory"} Formalization in structural set theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#membership_graphs"} Membership graphs
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#examples_of_apgs"} Examples of APGs
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ambiguities"} Ambiguities
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#membership_trees"} Membership trees
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#extensional_graphs"} Extensional graphs
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#equivalence"} Equivalence
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#motivation Motivation
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            is, which may be stated informally as follows: a set is a collection of sets. Actually, it is possible to have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} urelements
            in a material set theory (such as
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            %em pure
            set is a collection of pure sets.
          %p
            The primary motivation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            is that this conception of a set is not needed in ordinary
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            (although a structural set theory can also be described in ways other than category-theoretic). However, material set theory is itself part of mathematics, and we may want to describe the material notion of a pure set in structural terms.
          %p There is also a very practical point to this exercise: the translation between material and structural set theories. Any model of a material set theory is already a model of a corresponding structural set theory, but we go through the yoga below to construct a model of a material set theory out of a model of a structural set theory. In particular, we must do this to prove that two set theories (one material and one structural) are equiconsistent.
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material-structural+adjunction"} material-structural adjunction
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Taking a base notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            as granted, we wish to define a
            %strong pure set
            to be a set of pure sets. On the face of it, this is a circular definition, but like many such definitions, it can be made precise in (at least) two ways:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursively
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/corecursion"} corecursively
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The ‘recursive’ meaning is that all pure sets must be
                = succeed "," do
                  %em constructed
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
                    pure sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty = \{ \}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star = \{\empty\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo ⋆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star\}
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\empty,\star\}
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hereditarily+finite+sets"} hereditarily finite sets
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
                as well, we can jump to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/countably+infinite+set"} countably infinite set
                of all hereditarily finite sets and continue from there.
            %li
              %p
                The ‘corecursive’ meaning is that any pure set can be
                %em deconstructed
                into a set of other pure sets, and every possible such deconstruction defines a unique pure set. In this way we obtain not just well-founded pure sets but also
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong ill-founded pure sets
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/red+herring+principle"} red herring principle
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet = \{\bullet\}
                (a suggestive model for the
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \{B\}
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = \{\empty, A\}
          %p
            In addition, the meaning of this definition changes according to whether our
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set-theoretic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundation
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If the foundation is ‘material’ or ‘membership-based,’ such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
                %em be
                other sets. Therefore, in this case we are defining (either recursively or corecursively) an
                %em adjective
                “pure” that can be applied to the noun “set”: a set is pure if at no point in its construction or deconstruction into elements do we encounter anything that is not a pure set.
              %p
                In the most common material set theories, such as
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
                %em all
                sets are pure, since the only ‘things’ the theory deals with (hence the only things that can be elements of sets) are sets. However, there are easy modifications of these theories that allow ‘atoms’ or
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelement"} urelement
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
                asserting that all (pure) sets are well-founded; the dual
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+anti-foundation"} axiom of anti-foundation
                (due to Aczel) allows and ‘tames’ the ill-founded sets.
            %li
              %p
                If the foundation is ‘structural’ or ‘categorial’, such as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
                %em be
                other sets. Thus, in this case we are defining a single noun “pure set,” with no
                %em a priori
                relation to the structural notion of “set” that occurs in the foundational theory. A
                = succeed "," do
                  %em pure set
                %em equipped with structure
                assigning to each of its elements another pure set (interpreted either recursively or corecursively).
              %p
                From this point of view, the definition of pure set provides a construction of a model of material set theory within a model of structural set theory: a global relation of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                can be defined between pure sets which will satisfy the axioms of some material set theory. (Of course, the sets in a model of material set theory always model a structural set theory, so this is the ‘difficult’ direction of the equivalence between the two types of set theory.) Whether we use the recursive or corecursive definition determines whether the resulting material set theory will satisfy the axiom of foundation or the axiom of anti-foundation. Note that using these two choices, the composite operation “material
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                structural
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                material” reduces to the standard proofs of the relative consistency of the axioms of foundation and anti-foundation, since the passage from material to structural requires neither one.
          %h2#formalisation_in_material_set_theory Formalisation in material set theory
          %p In material set theory without urelements, every set is a pure set. If there are urelements, so that not all sets are pure, then it is easy to define the class of pure sets as follows:
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is
              %strong pure
              if given any finite sequence
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi …
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n \in x_{n-1} \in \dots \in x_2 \in x_1 \in x_0 = x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
              are sets.
          %p Whether this produces the well-founded pure sets or the ill-founded ones depends on whether the sets in the ambient set theory are well-founded or ill-founded.
          %p If you are (unusually) working in an ill-founded material set theory and want only the well-founded pure sets, then you simply need to define the other adjective:
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              of sets is
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
                \-inductive
              if whenever all elements of some set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              itself is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is
              %strong well-founded
              if it belongs to every
              = succeed "-inductive" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            (That
            %em all
            sets are well-founded is the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is well-founded, then there can be no infinite sequence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi …
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in \dots \in x_2 \in x_1 \in x_0 = x
            = succeed "-inductive" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p If you are (as usual) working in a well-founded material set theory and want to work with ill-founded pure sets, then you cannot do so directly; instead, follow the structural development in the next section.
          %h2#formalization_in_structural_set_theory Formalization in structural set theory
          %p
            In a structural set theory like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
          %p
            One way to state the basic theoretical idea is that the class of well-founded sets is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra"} initial algebra
            of the covariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            functor, while the class of ill-founded sets is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} terminal coalgebra
            of the same functor. Of course, neither of these algebras exists as a set, since this would violate
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cantor%27s+theorem"} Cantor's theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
          %p
            The discussion which follows is phrased informally, like most mathematics. However, it is purely structural and can be interpreted in any structural set theory. For instance, the definition below of a “graph” as a set with a binary relation should be formalized as a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            together with an injection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \hookrightarrow G \times G
          %h3#membership_graphs Membership graphs
          %p
            To describe a pure set, we must give all the elements of that set, each of which is a pure set and thus must have its own elements, which are also pure sets, and so on. A convenient way to “picture” a pure set is with a
            = succeed "." do
              %em graph
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              For the purposes of this page, a
              %strong graph
              will mean a set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              of
              %strong nodes
              equipped with a binary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              on the nodes. A node
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is called a
              %strong child
              of a node
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo →
                      %mi j
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \to j
          %p
            The idea of a graph-picture of pure sets is that the
            %em nodes
            represent
            = succeed "," do
              %em pure sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\to j
            precisely when
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\in j
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+anti-foundation"} axiom of anti-foundation
            is that any graph is the picture of some pure set.)
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              of nodes in a graph is
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                \-inductive
              if whenever all children of some node
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              are in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              itself is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-founded+relation"} well-founded
              if the only
              = succeed "-inductive" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
          %p
            Assuming the principle of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
          %p
            If we want to use a graph to describe a
            %em specific
            pure set, then we should single out one particular node as representing
            %em that
            set. Moreover, it makes sense to demand that the graph contain no “superfluous” data other than what is necessary to describe the hereditary membership structure of the particular set in question.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              A graph is
              %strong pointed
              if it is equipped with a specified node
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              called the
              = succeed "." do
                %strong root
              %strong accessible
              if for every node
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = x_0 \to x_1 \to \dots \to x_n =\top
              to the root; this is equivalent to saying that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/top+element"} top element
              for the reflexive-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+closure"} transitive closure
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
              = succeed "." do
                %strong APG
          %p
            An APG gives all the data necessary to characterize a pure set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            itself, the root's children as the elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            If we want to think of these elements as pure sets themselves, then we can “externalize” them into separate APGs. Specifically, for any node
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            in a graph
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            is an APG, which describes the pure set represented by
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/z
            and call it the
            %strong full subgraph
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            rooted at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is an APG and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            is a child of the root, we say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/z
            is an
            %strong immediate subgraph
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            represent the pure sets that are the “elements” of the pure set represented by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            pictures the hierarchy of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/z
            corresponds to its root
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            is interpreted as membership.
          %h3#examples_of_apgs Examples of APGs
          %p
            Note that the nodes in most of the pictures below have labels, but these are for illustration only; they are
            %em not
            part of the structure defining the tree.
          %p
            First,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty = \{ \}
            consists of nothing but a root:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
          %p
            The set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi ∅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star = \{\empty\}
            has the empty set attached to the root (which we draw at the top):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array { \star \\ \uparrow \\ \empty }
          %p
            Notice that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
            is an immediate subgraph, so we can write
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo ⋆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \in \star
          %p
            Then the two most obvious ways of representing the ordinal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            as a pure set, namely
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_Z = \{\star\}
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi N
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N = \{\empty, \star\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi Z
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array { 2_Z \\ \uparrow \\ \star \\ \uparrow \\ \empty } \quad \quad \quad \quad \array {
                  \&amp;          &amp; 2_N \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;     &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\empty &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \star    \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \uparrow \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \empty
                  }
          %p
            Looking at immediate subtrees,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star \in 2_Z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \in 2_N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star \in 2_N
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ∉
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \notin 2_Z
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N
            can also be represented as
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star, \empty\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp;          &amp; 2_N \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;     &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\star    &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \empty \\
                  \\uparrow \\
                  \\empty
                  }
          %p
            Of course, as a graph this is isomorphic to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\empty, \star\}
          %p
            These sets are all well-founded. A non-well-founded set can be represented either by an infinite picture, or by a picture with loops. Here is an infinite picture of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet = \{\bullet\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array { \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \vdots }
          %p and here is a finite picture:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo \
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp; \bullet \\
                  \/          &amp;         &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\backslash &amp;         &amp; /        \\
                  }
          %p
            In both cases, the unique immediate subgraphs verify that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet \in \bullet
          %h3#ambiguities Ambiguities
          %p
            APGs are sufficient to describe all pure sets, but multiple APGs, even non-isomorphic ones, can represent the “same” pure set. Firstly, we have so far not eliminated the possibility of duplicate branches. For instance, the pure set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_Z
            can also be represented as
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\star, \star\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi Z
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp;          &amp; 2_Z \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;     &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\star    &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \star    \\
                  \\uparrow &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \uparrow \\
                  \\empty   &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \empty
                  }
          %p It is easy to eliminate APGs with such “obviously redundant” branches.
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              An APG
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is
              %strong rigid
              if whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              are two children of the same node
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x \cong G/y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=y
          %p
            Assuming
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism"} automorphism
            is the identity function. Note also that if two rigid APGs are isomorphic, they must be uniquely isomorphic. Thus, for rigid APGs, being isomorphic is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em property
            = succeed "." do
              %em structure
          %p
            Even among rigid APGs, however, there are are ambiguities. For example, the pure set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N
            can also be drawn by the APG
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  2_N \\
                  \\uparrow &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  |        &amp;          &amp; \star    \\
                  |        &amp; \nearrow &amp;  \\
                  \\empty   &amp;          &amp;
                  }
          %p The issue here is that the “same” pure set can occur in more than one place in the membership hierarchy of a pure set, and we have to choose whether or not to identify them in the graph. There are three solutions to this problem:
          %ol
            %li Demand that duplicate occurrences of a pure set always be represented separately.
            %li Demand that duplicate occurrences always be identified, wherever they occur.
            %li Define a notion of “equivalence” between APGs which is looser than isomorphism.
          %p
            These are of course related; the first two can be seen as specifying a particular isomorphism class within each of the equivalence classes defined by the third. In particular, they all result in equivalent notions of pure set, when they all apply. (However, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            set theories, it seems that only the first method is applicable.) We will consider them all in turn.
          %h3#membership_trees Membership trees
          %p
            A natural way to ensure that the same node is never “shared” between two occurrences is to require that our APGs be
            = succeed "." do
              %em trees
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_7 Definition
            %p
              An pointed graph is a
              %strong tree
              if every node admits a
              %em unique
              path to the root. Equivalently,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is a tree if its root
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quiver
              generated by the relation
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo →
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
          %p
            A tree may or may not be well-founded. Note, though, that in a tree, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+relation"} opposite relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ←
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftarrow
            is automatically well-founded: each node has a unique
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            = succeed "," do
              %em height
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            implies well-foundedness of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ←
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftarrow
          %p
            It now makes sense to define a
            %em pure set
            to be a
            = succeed "." do
              %em rigid accessible pointed tree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\in H
            between such trees to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is isomorphic to some immediate subtree of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            (that is, a tree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/z
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            is a child of the root of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
          %p
            For example, the tree representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N
            is the one we gave first:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp;          &amp; 2_N \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp;     &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\empty &amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \star    \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \uparrow \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;     &amp;          &amp; \empty
                  }
          %p
            and the tree representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet = \{\bullet\}
            is the infinite path:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                          %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array { \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \bullet \\ \uparrow \\ \vdots .}
          %p
            Of course, a well-founded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+set"} infinite set
            also has an infinite picture, but there are no infinite paths. Here is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_N
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋮
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp;          &amp;          &amp; \omega_N \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp; \nearrow &amp;          &amp; \nwarrow &amp; \cdots \\
                  \\empty  &amp; \star    &amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp; 2_N      &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp; \uparrow &amp;          &amp;          &amp; \nearrow &amp; \uparrow &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp; \empty   &amp;          &amp; \empty   &amp;          &amp; \star    &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp; \uparrow &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp; \empty   &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp;          &amp; \vdots
                  }
          %p
            So we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \in \omega_N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star \in \omega_N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi N
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N \in \omega
          %p
            A tree models a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hereditarily+finite+set"} hereditarily finite set
            if every node has finitely many children; a tree models a well-founded hereditarily finite set if and only if it is finite and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidable+subset"} decidable
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Kőnig's Lemma
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Konig%27s+lemma"} ?
          %p
            Given these definitions, one can prove the various axioms of material set theory. Specifically, if
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            holds for the structural sets (of nodes, etc) used in this definition, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi BZC
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BZC}^-
            (which is
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            with only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+separation"} bounded separation
            and no axioms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} replacement
            or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} foundation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi BZC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BZC}
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi BZC
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BZC}^-
            together with foundation) holds for well-founded rigid trees. Similar results hold for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            weakenings of ETCS and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi BZC
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{BZC}^-
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universes"} Grothendieck universes
          %p
            The argument for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
            is perhaps the most interesting; it goes as follows. If each immediate subtree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is isomorphic to an immediate subtree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/y
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            and conversely, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            are mutually uniquely determined by rigidity, so we have a bijection between the children of the roots of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x \cong H/y
            are also unique, again by rigidity, and each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x
            is disjoint from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x'
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi x
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\neq x'
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\cong H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            is not needed.)
          %p
            (Note, though, that this is only the “weak” version of extensionality. This is sufficient for well-founded sets, but we may need to strengthen rigidity somehow to obtain stronger notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensionality
          %h3#extensional_graphs Extensional graphs
          %p
            The “tree” representation can be thought of as an
            %em unwrapped
            picture; we now consider a
            %em wrapped
            picture in which there is no duplication at all. The natural way to ensure this is to require our graphs to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional
            %em extensional
            if for any nodes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            such that for all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\to x
            iff
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z\to y
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=y
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_8 Definition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              are graphs, a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bisimulation"} bisimulation
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              is a binary relation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              such that for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in G
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in H
              with
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\sim y
            %ul
              %li
                For any
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in H
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\to y
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\sim b
              %li
                For any
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo →
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\to y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in G
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\to x
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\sim b
            %p
              A graph
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is
              %strong extensional
              if the largest bisimulation from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is the identity.
          %p
            The idea is that a bisimulation identifies pairs of nodes which “represent the same pure set.” One can show that a well-founded graph is extensional in this sense iff it is extensional in the weaker sense given above. Note also that any extensional APG is necessarily rigid, since any isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x \cong G/y
            induces a bisimulation.
          %p
            Thus, it also makes sense to define a
            %em pure set
            to be an
            = succeed "." do
              %em extensional APG
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\in H
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\cong H/x
            for some child
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of the root of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantification
            over bisimulations, which are relations, i.e. subsets. Thus, it requires at least
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+separation"} limited separation
          %p
            For example, the extensional representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 2
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N
            is this one:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  2_N \\
                  \\uparrow &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  |        &amp;          &amp; \star    \\
                  |        &amp; \nearrow &amp;  \\
                  \\empty   &amp;          &amp;
                  }
          %p
            while the extensional representation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet = \{\bullet \}
            is the loop:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo •
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo \
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp; \bullet \\
                  \/          &amp;         &amp; \nwarrow \\
                  \\backslash &amp;         &amp; /        \\
                  }
          %p
            Finally, here is the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_N
            of von Neumann
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ω
                            %mi N
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↖
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mn 2
                            %mi N
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋆
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo \
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mtd
                          %mi ⋯
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array {
                  \&amp;            &amp; \omega_N \\
                  \&amp; \nearrow   &amp; \uparrow &amp; \nwarrow &amp; \cdots \\
                  |        &amp;            &amp; |        &amp;          &amp; 2_N      &amp; \cdots \\
                  |        &amp;            &amp; |        &amp; \nearrow &amp; \uparrow &amp; \cdots \\
                  |        &amp;            &amp; \star    &amp;          &amp; |        &amp; \cdots \\
                  \&amp; \backslash &amp; \uparrow &amp; /        &amp; \cdots   \\
                  \&amp;            &amp; \empty
                  }
          %p
            Again we have
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \in \omega_N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star \in \omega_N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn 2
                      %mi N
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2_N \in \omega
          %p
            Now an extensional accessible graph models a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hereditarily+finite+set"} hereditarily finite set
            iff the graph itself is finite and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            is decidable.
          %h3#equivalence Equivalence
          %p
            Finally, we consider the third solution to the ambiguity problem, and its relation to the first two: defining a looser notion of equivalence between APGs. Recall the notion of bisimulation. We say that two APGs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            are
            %strong equivalent
            if there is a bisimulation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊤
                    %mo ∼
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \top\sim\top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            is a bisimulation from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\sim y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/y
            are equivalent; this justifies the interpretation of a bisimulation.
          %p
            We can now define a
            %em pure set
            to be an APG, with
            %em equality
            represented by
            %em equivalence
            in the above sense. Likewise, we define “membership”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\in H
            to mean that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is
            %em equivalent
            to some immediate subgraph of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %em strong
            notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensionality
            (which is equivalent to the weak one for well-founded sets). This definition is also not predicative.
          %p The relationship of this approach to the previous ones is as follows.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Every APG is equivalent to an extensional one. Namely, let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \approx
                be the maximal bisimulation on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                (the union of all bisimulations); it is an equivalence relation, and its quotient
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ≈
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/\approx
                inherits the structure of an extensional APG which is equivalent to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em extensional quotient
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensional+relation"} extensional relation
            %li
              %p
                Two extensional APGs are equivalent iff they are isomorphic. For if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                is a bisimulation from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≈
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\approx y
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                if there exists a node
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\sim z
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\sim z
                gives a bisimulation on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                must in fact be a bijection. Thus, the notion of “pure set” obtained from “extensional APGs and isomorphism” is the same as that obtained from “arbitrary APGs and equivalence.”
            %li
              %p
                Every APG is equivalent to one that is a tree, its “unwrapping”. The nodes of the tree can be taken to be the finite paths
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi …
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n \to \dots \to x_0 = \top
                ending at the root, with any “one-level extension”
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi …
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{n+1} \to x_n \to \dots \to \top
                being a child of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mi …
                        %mo →
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n \to \dots\to \top
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %mi …
                      %mo →
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n \to \dots\to \top
                to its initial node
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_n
            %li
              %p
                Moreover, every APG is equivalent to a
                %em rigid
                accessible pointed tree. The key here is to first take the extensional quotient, then perform the above “unwrapping” construction: the unwrapping of any extensional graph will always be rigid.
            %li
              %p
                Two well-founded rigid accessible pointed trees are equivalent iff they are isomorphic. For if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                is a bisimulation from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\simeq y
                iff there are paths from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                to the corresponding roots which are pointwise related by
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∼
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi ⋯
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = x_n \to x_{n-1} \to \cdots \to x_1 \to x_0 = \top
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %mi ⋯
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mn 1
                        %mo →
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mo ⊤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = y_n \to y_{n-1} \to \cdots \to y_1 \to y_0 = \top
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∼
                        %msub
                          %mi y
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i \sim y_i
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\simeq y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                is a bijection between the children of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                have this property. Then if we have
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' \to x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ″
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x''\to x
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∼
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x' \sim y
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ″
                        %mo ∼
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'' \sim y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∼
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                is a bijection between
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x'
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo ″
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x''
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H/y
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi x
                        %mo ″
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/x' \cong G/x''
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                        %mo ″
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'=x''
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                is a bijection between the children of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and the children of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                then ensures that for any child
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ′
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x'\to x
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                relates each node in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/x'
                to a node in at most one
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi y
                        %mo ′
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y/y'
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                is in fact a bijection from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/x
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y/y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≃
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
                is a bijection.
          %p In order to extend this last result to non-well-founded trees, we would need to use a stronger notion of rigidity. (This makes sense since SAFA is incompatible with AFA. We could expect to be able to use “Scott-extensionality” in place of extensionality to obtain a theory equivalent to that of rigid trees.) For example, the following two ill-founded trees are equivalent but not isomorphic, yet both are rigid.
          .maruku-equation
